{"name":"Jasper Red","hex":"#fa2b00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jasper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any bright-coloured kind of chalcedony apart from cornelian.","An opaque, impure variety of quartz, of red, yellow, and other dull colors, breaking conchoidally with a smooth surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with, or as if with, jasper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jasper"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jasper","blocks":[{"text":"Jasper is an opaque type of silica that comes in a variety of colors, most commonly red, yellow, brown, green, and very rarely blue. It is made mostly of very fine-grained quartz or chalcedony, with other minerals contributing to its color and appearance. The common reddish color is due to iron inclusions. Jasper breaks with a smooth surface and is used for ornamentation or as a gemstone. It can be highly polished and is used for items such as vases, jewelry, seals, and snuff boxes. The density of jasper is typically 2.5 to 2.9 g/cm3. Jaspillite is a banded-iron-formation rock that often has distinctive bands of jasper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Jasper_outcrop_in_the_Bucegi_Mountains.jpg/330px-Jasper_outcrop_in_the_Bucegi_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasper","wikidata":"Q201175"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Movable Egyptian ring in green jasper and gold, from 664 to 322 BC or later (Late Period),[5] the Walters Art Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgyptian_-_Finger_Ring_with_a_Representation_of_Ptah_-_Walters_42387_-_Side_A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG/500px-Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.JPG/1280px-Red_jasper_amulet_HARGM7392.J